== (1) D21: closed form for cs(D(G)) = omega(D^2(G)) ==
 omega(D^2(G)) = closed-form 4-part maximum on all labeled graphs with 2 <= n <= 5 (1098 graphs): True
 ... and on 60 random graphs with n = 6 and 15 with n = 7: True
== (2) D22: Lehmer conductors against factorials ==
 factorial hits for |n| <= 60: [(-2, 'f(n)^2 - 1', '5!'), (-1, 'f(n)^2 - 1', '5!'), (0, 'f(n) - 1', '4!'), (1, 'f(n)^2 - 1', '7!')] (the three Brocard cases and nothing else)
== (3) the Lehmer five and the sequence of 276 ==
 Lehmer five: [276, 552, 564, 660, 966] factorizations: [{2: 2, 3: 1, 23: 1}, {2: 3, 3: 1, 23: 1}, {2: 2, 3: 1, 47: 1}, {2: 2, 3: 1, 5: 1, 11: 1}, {2: 1, 3: 1, 7: 1, 23: 1}]
 the sequence of 138 returns to 1 after 177 steps with peak 179931895322 (exponent log(peak)/log(138) = 5.26; Collatz record excursions have exponent below 1.9)
 Guy-Selfridge driver test (2^a v, v | 2^(a+1)-1, 2^(a-1) | sigma(v)): {2: True, 6: True, 24: True, 28: True, 120: True, 496: True, 672: True, 8128: True, 523776: True}
 so 4! = 24 and 5! = 120 are drivers; 7! = 5040 = 2^4 * 315 is not (315 does not divide 31)
